WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
OpenAI's first custom inference chip is entering production, delivering higher throughput and lower latency in one architecture. This is Models &amp; Agents, Episode 153. OpenAI plans to deploy its jalapeno inference chip into production clusters by year end. Early tests show the design improves both throughput and latency at once, raising intelligence per watt for chat GPT and agent workloads. A new chat GPT business premium seats plan

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:54.700
launches at $100 per seat for small teams. Quen 3.8 FlashNext reaches release today with weights already posted on hugging face and model scope. Liquid AI open sourced the pipette benchmarking suite for on-device model evaluation. OpenAI plans to begin deploying jalapeno, its first custom inference chip into production compute infrastructure by year end.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:14.500
Testing results indicate the architecture delivers both higher throughput and lower latency without efficiency trade-offs. This setup supports faster chat GPT responses, more responsive codex sessions and agents under growing demand. Gen 2 is already deep in development and Gen 3 is taking shape.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:26.840
The company also introduced chat GPT business premium seats at $100 per seat. The plan targets small businesses and startups that need advanced tools previously limited to large enterprises.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:38.800
Remember we covered AI compute and inference yesterday. Today's jalapeno deployment moves the inference cost curve forward by integrating custom silicon into existing clusters first.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:50.480
Quen 3.8 FlashNext arrives today with an estimated release at 1,500 UTC. Official weights are posted on hugging face and model scope for immediate download.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:02:02.560
Community discussion centers on quants, fine tunes, chat templates and inference server support. The model targets multimodal and high speed use cases following recent quen family releases.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:22.380
A fully NVFP-4 quantized Quen 3.8-27B checkpoint was released after 2446 steps of quantization-aware distillation. The model runs in VLLM on Nvidia Blackwell GPUs with a 262,144 token context.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:41.320
It retains near BF-16 performance on GPQA diamond and AIME-26 despite aggressive W4A4 quantization across all linear layers. Attention and GDN layers stay at higher precision because full quantization there causes larger quality loss.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:58.540
The checkpoint sits at Quasar-QAT-QN 3.8-27B-Quasar-NVFP-4 and supports the VLLM serve command with max model length 262,144.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:09.240
IBM released Granite 4.2, a family of open reasoning models in 3 billion, 8 billion and 30 billion sizes, under Apache 2.0.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:26.440
Every model exposes a thinking, low effort or non-thinking switch plus native tool calling. The 8 billion and 30 billion variants receive additional agentic reinforcement learning training for code editing, terminal use and web search inside sandboxed environments.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:34.960
The 30 billion model reports 57 on sweet bench verified and 29.24 on terminal bench 2.1.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:42.780
SSE Bio maintains a structured state and uses a trainable proxy policy with group relative policy optimization.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:50.900
The agent decides retrieval of knowledge triplets and prior templates, improving reasoning memory through fine-grained template editing.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:04:01.220
On three biomedical multi-hop QA benchmarks, it outperforms the strongest self-evolving baseline by 6.56 absolute points on BioHOP R.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:07.720
A dual-layer agentic memory framework routes incoming information through a small to large model cascade.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:16.440
The cascade labels each item non-write, write new, or write update, pruning up to 68% of redundant external memory.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:26.420
High-value memories later consolidate into parameters via supervised fine-tuning while retaining over 98% of downstream QA exact match.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:35.400
Paul Dick's noted that AI wrote and iteratively refined 1 million lines of code into reliable software, now running on millions of machines.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:41.160
Verification systems and clear direction enabled the outcome even with an Oracle for comparison.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.460
Liquid AI open-socused pipette, a benchmarking suite developed with artificial analysis.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.620
The suite measures on-device model quality, quantization, runtime, and hardware together.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.500
It addresses the gap between server-class model cards and real-phone behavior.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:08.080
Try the Quasar QAT NVFP4 QN3.8-27B checkpoint in VLLM on Blackwell GPUs.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:14.040
If you need 27 billion class performance at roughly one-third the memory footprint of BF-16.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:22.760
Benchmark your edge models with the newly open-sourced pipette suite to see how quantization and runtime choices behave on target hardware.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:33.100
Test Quin 3.8-flash-next once released against current 8 billion open models on agent tool-calling tasks where speed matters more than peak capability.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:44.700
Run the SSE BioProxy Policy Training Loop on a small biomedical multi-hop data set to see whether structured state plus fine-grained template editing reduces instruction drift.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:54.220
Compare the granite 4.2-30 billion models' agentic reinforcement learning results on suite-bunch verified against your current open-coding agents.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.620
Gen 2 of OpenAI's inference chip roadmap is already in deep development.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:06.180
Quin 3.8-flash-next community quants and fine-tunes are expected within days of today's release.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:12.300
More on-device benchmarking results using pipette will likely surface as edge teams adopt the suite.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.800
Everyone talks about custom inference chips as a simple efficiency win.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:23.760
In practice, the design centers on balancing throughput, latency, and power within a fixed thermal envelope.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:32.420
Halipaneos reported gains come from a single architecture that raises both throughput and lowers latency rather than trading one for the other.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:41.620
The first production step is integration into existing OpenAI clusters, which limits initial risk while the team validates system-level behavior.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:51.360
Later generations will target further efficiency and speed, but each step must still clear the same power and cooling constraints that limit GPU clusters today.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:07:02.640
The practical decision for teams is whether the resulting token-cost curve justifies moving workloads onto the new silicon once access expands beyond OpenAI's own services.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:13.240
When your workload is dominated by high-volume, latency-sensitive inference, and you already sit inside OpenAI's ecosystem, the chip becomes worth testing first.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:19.500
Before we go, early signals from OpenAI's Gen2 inference chip should surface later this year.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.760
That's models and agents for today.

